#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073642 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073745 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e25f87c9-3f2e-59a6-ad45-66f0640e68ef" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cubic Equation With No Constant Term"}] :question/id #uuid "35be9962-fbfd-57f5-906e-41660079063d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5171" :question/problem "Solve the equation $2x^3 - 6x^2 + 4x= 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $2x.$ We can factor out the $2x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+4x & =0 \\\\ 2x({x}^{2}-3x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $x^2-3x+2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x({x}^{2}-3x+2) & =0 \\\\ 2x(x-1)(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $x = 0,$ $x = 1,$ and $x = 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073643 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073746 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f2d12ff-f760-5c03-88c0-2b7088eb5fb2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cubic Equation by Rearranging"}] :question/id #uuid "ccefedb4-9e45-56c4-bb11-460ed8fe1793" :question/math-academy-id "e-5172" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $x^3 - x^2 - x = 3x^2 + 4x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form $P_n(x)=0.$ To do this, we subtract all the terms from the right-hand side so that the right-hand side becomes $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-x & =3{x}^{2}+4x \\\\ {x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-x & =4x \\\\ {x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $x.$ We can factor out the $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-4x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $x^2 - 4x - 5$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x({x}^{2}-4x-5) & =0 \\\\ x(x+1)(x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $x = 0,$ $x = -1,$ and $x = 5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073644 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073747 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3974b5c6-129f-5616-be84-08b6ab779f70" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Higher-Order Polynomial Equation With No Constant Term"}] :question/id #uuid "830e79ed-49ac-537f-aa67-9bfd063bb258" :question/math-academy-id "e-5173" :question/problem "If $3x^5 + x^3 = 3x^3 - 5x^4,$ then what are the values of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form $P_n(x)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{5}+{x}^{3} & =3{x}^{3}-5{x}^{4} \\\\ 3{x}^{5}+5{x}^{4}+{x}^{3} & =3{x}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{5}+5{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $x^3.$ We can factor out the $x^3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{5}+5{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(3{x}^{2}+5x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $3x^2+5x-2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}(3{x}^{2}+5x-2) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(3x-1)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $x = 0,$ $x = \\dfrac{1}{3},$ and $x = -2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233206 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073747 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3974b5c6-129f-5616-be84-08b6ab779f70" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Higher-Order Polynomial Equation With No Constant Term"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d6db630c-4e99-5e1f-8d98-caea7db3fdf0" :question/math-academy-id "q-114111" :question/problem "If $3{h}^{5}-32{h}^{4}-4{h}^{3}=16{h}^{3}-13{h}^{5},$ then what are the values of $h?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form ${P}_{n}(t)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{h}^{5}-32{h}^{4}-4{h}^{3} & =16{h}^{3}-13{h}^{5} \\\\ 16{h}^{5}-32{h}^{4}-4{h}^{3} & =16{h}^{3} \\\\ 16{h}^{5}-32{h}^{4}-20{h}^{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $4{h}^{3}.$ We can factor out the $4{h}^{3}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{h}^{5}-32{h}^{4}-20{h}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 4{h}^{3}(4{h}^{2}-8h-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $4{h}^{2}-8h-5$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{h}^{3}(4{h}^{2}-8h-5) & =0 \\\\ 4{h}^{3}(2h+1)(2h-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $h=0,$ $h=-\\frac{1}{2},$ and $h=\\frac{5}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073747 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3974b5c6-129f-5616-be84-08b6ab779f70" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Higher-Order Polynomial Equation With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09e9fedc-4d5e-5788-be94-10e0f727eb89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=-\\frac{3}{2},0,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431362} {:answer/id #uuid "0b30b2b8-aa8f-5c31-b7e3-bc987c2700fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=-2,0,\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431364} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2e1eb1-9a53-5789-b781-a03ef2aeb0b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=0,\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431414} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b64a4c-411a-532f-abeb-5237ce881e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=0,\\frac{5}{3},2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431427} {:answer/id #uuid "f01b3681-4519-57fb-bc76-853ded546956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=0,\\frac{3}{4},1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f01b3681-4519-57fb-bc76-853ded546956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=0,\\frac{3}{4},1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431429} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1caed9b-00a5-576f-9db3-b95772341ed6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "af7e750a-c93b-50d4-aa96-76ae41484ef4" :question/math-academy-id "q-114116" :question/problem "Find the roots of the polynomial $p(w)=60{w}^{6}-105{w}^{5}+45{w}^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the roots of the polynomial, we must solve the equation $p(w)=0,$ that is\n\n$$\n60{w}^{6}-105{w}^{5}+45{w}^{4}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $15{w}^{4}.$ We can factor out the $15{w}^{4}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15{w}^{4}(4{w}^{2}-7w+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $4{w}^{2}-7w+3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15{w}^{4}(4{w}^{2}-7w+3) & =0 \\\\ 15{w}^{4}(4w-3)(w-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $w=0,$ $w=\\frac{3}{4},$ and $w=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073745 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e25f87c9-3f2e-59a6-ad45-66f0640e68ef" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cubic Equation With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5059480d-49b5-5e4a-bd13-82ce2b77ee01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-6,\\,0,\\,\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431384} {:answer/id #uuid "5713fbbf-27f4-5c95-b6fd-bfb010b612f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,0,\\,\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431387} {:answer/id #uuid "6b8251d7-0c1c-57d3-82c1-96e020e790ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-\\frac{5}{2},\\,0,\\,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431392} {:answer/id #uuid "7033358f-af7b-5515-ab10-9200cdcc75f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=0,\\,5,\\,6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431394} {:answer/id #uuid "e4d9cfdf-e3f3-5aa1-80a4-8f44ea2ba2c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-\\frac{5}{2},\\,0,\\,6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4d9cfdf-e3f3-5aa1-80a4-8f44ea2ba2c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-\\frac{5}{2},\\,0,\\,6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431426} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ddd1011-6a73-5b1e-91ae-c188d140fc65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f41bc86-fed5-5013-8ded-0ffbd4097db1" :question/math-academy-id "q-14008" :question/problem "Solve the equation $6{z}^{3}-21{z}^{2}-90z=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $3z.$ We can factor out the $3z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{z}^{3}-21{z}^{2}-90z & =0 \\\\ 3z(2{z}^{2}-7z-30) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $2{z}^{2}-7z-30$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z(2{z}^{2}-7z-30) & =0 \\\\ 3z(2z+5)(z-6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $z=0,$ $z=-\\frac{5}{2},$ and $z=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073745 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e25f87c9-3f2e-59a6-ad45-66f0640e68ef" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cubic Equation With No Constant Term"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de2e4bb1-71bc-5d04-acd2-ad307f10ba69" :question/math-academy-id "q-71778" :question/problem "Solve the equation $5{z}^{3}-35{z}^{2}+50z=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $5z.$ We can factor out the $5z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{z}^{3}-35{z}^{2}+50z & =0 \\\\ 5z({z}^{2}-7z+10) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression ${z}^{2}-7z+10$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5z({z}^{2}-7z+10) & =0 \\\\ 5z(z-2)(z-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $z=0,$ $z=2,$ and $z=5.$"}]}
